# Runtime worktree state
# ⚠ `/set/*`, not `/set/`. A trailing-slash exclusion excludes the DIRECTORY, and git
# never descends into an excluded directory — so the negation below would have had no
# effect at all, silently. Measured here: `git add` kept refusing the file with the
# negation already in place.
/set/*
# …except the work-cycle ADOPTION DECLARATION. `/set/` is ignored because it holds
# runtime state, and the engine's declaration happens to live in the same place —
# so an adopted project is adopted only on the machine that adopted it, and a
# clone silently is not. Measured here on 2026-08-29: `git add set/work-cycle.yaml`
# was refused, which is the polite version of the failure; the quiet version is a
# project that reads as unadopted for everybody else.
!/set/work-cycle.yaml

